// @test regression/INV162-argument-out-of-documented-range
// @description A numeric LITERAL outside the range the reference documents for that parameter. TradingView compiles all of these (probed 2026-08-25: `color.rgb(300, 0, 0)` and `color.new(color.red, 150)` are both clean at `--tv`), so this is a `lint`-stage warning and never an error. The in-range cases carry the rule's whole false-positive story and are why the count is the assertion: the BOUNDARIES are legal and must stay silent, and a non-literal argument is undecidable and must stay silent too - a runtime value cannot be shown to violate anything. The domain is not a table in the checker: `min`/`max` come from functions.json, parsed at generate-time from each parameter's own prose, so a parameter with no documented range is skipped. See INV162, TODO #69.
// @expects parse: success
// @expects errors: 0
// @expects warnings: 3
// @expects warning: line=4, message=/documents 'red' as 0 to 255, but 300 was passed/
// @expects warning: line=5, message=/documents 'transp' as 0 to 100, but 150 was passed/
// @expects warning: line=6, message=/documents 'red' as 0 to 255, but -1 was passed/
//@version=6
indicator("argument out of documented range")

over = color.rgb(300, 0, 0)
transp = color.new(color.red, 150)
under = color.rgb(-1, 0, 0)

// In range, including both boundaries - must stay silent.
lowEdge = color.rgb(0, 0, 0)
highEdge = color.rgb(255, 255, 255)
transpEdge = color.new(color.red, 100)

// Not a literal, so not decidable - must stay silent whatever the input says.
runtimeValue = input.int(999, "transparency")
fromInput = color.new(color.red, runtimeValue)

plot(1, color = over)
plot(2, color = transp)
plot(3, color = under)
plot(4, color = lowEdge)
plot(5, color = highEdge)
plot(6, color = transpEdge)
plot(7, color = fromInput)
